eight=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: Big gyms go boutique </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td> <img src=\"https://images.axios.com/_qQgGVbEEn03Xyx48eK8zzJiLwQ=/0x0:1920x1080/1920x1080/2026/09/14/1789396154327.jpeg\" width=\"600\" style=\"width:100%\" id=\"item02\" alt=\"Illustration of different types of gym and workout equipment in a vending machine. \"> </td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.3\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#848484\"> <p>Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images</p> </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"15\" style=\"height:15px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><p><strong>Boutique fitness</strong> is going mainstream.</p><ul><li>🧘  <strong>The specialized experiences </strong>that once required a separate studio — Pilates, yoga, and recovery services and equipment — are increasingly showing up inside traditional gyms, <em>Axios' Natalie Daher writes.</em></li></ul><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> After years of fitness splintering into ever-more-specialized studios and memberships, gyms are borrowing the boutique playbook and putting more of the experience under one roof.</p><ul><li><strong>Think Pilates and yoga</strong> alongside the weight room and treadmills, plus cold plunges, infrared saunas and compression therapy.</li><li><strong>The goal</strong> is more variety without the boutique price tag.</li></ul><p><strong>💪 Case in point:</strong> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47610346.373567/aHR0cHM6Ly9hbXBlZGZpdG5lc3MuY29tLz91dG1fc291cmNlPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXImdXRtX21lZGl1bT1lbWFpbCZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249bmV3c2xldHRlcl9heGlvc2ZpbmlzaGxpbmUmc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB82fa46e2\" target=\"_blank\">Amped Fitness</a>, a fast-growing gym chain, now offers roughly 25 Pilates and yoga classes a week at some locations alongside other recovery amenities.</p><ul><li><strong>CEO and co-founder Travis LaBazzo</strong> told Axios the company wants to \"flip\" the economics of boutique fitness: \"We think we can give people a phenomenal product and a phenomenal experience\" at a lower cost. </li><li><strong>He traces that philosophy</strong> to his days as a personal trainer, when he saw firsthand how often the cost kept people from meeting their health and fitness goals.</li><li><strong>Demand has been</strong> <strong>strong</strong> enough, he said, that Amped is considering an unlimited-access tier for classes.</li></ul><p>🔄 <strong>The big picture:</strong> The magazine Women's Health <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47610346.373567/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cud29tZW5zaGVhbHRobWFnLmNvbS9maXRuZXNzL2E3MzQ3Njc3NS9ib3V0aXF1ZS1zdHVkaW9zLWd5bXMtcmVidW5kbGluZy10cmVuZC8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB611df11b\" target=\"_blank\">recently dubbed</a> the broader shift the \"great rebundling\" of fitness.</p><ul><li><strong>Crunch Fitness </strong><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47610346.\" target=\"_blank\">launched</a> a reformer Pilates studio concept across at least 20 locations this year, while <strong>LifeTime </strong>has also <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47610346.\" target=\"_blank\">been expanding</a> its reformer footprint. Reformer pilates uses a sliding machine with springs and straps for low-impact resistance training.</li></ul><p><strong>Between the lines:</strong> The workout mix is shifting, too. The Atlantic recently <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47610346.373567/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cudGhlYXRsYW50aWMuY29tL2N1bHR1cmUvMjAyNi8wOC95b2dhLWFtZXJpY2EtY3VsdHVyZS1kZWNsaW5lLzY4ODQ2MS8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB0c28fb40\" target=\"_blank\">chronicled yoga's fading</a> cultural dominance as Pilates and strength training gain ground.</p><ul><li><strong>And Pilates is broadening</strong> its audience: The Wall Street Journal reports that more \"gym bros\" are joining partners and friends for classes, including one powerlifter who declared afterward: \"I'm a new man.\" He's all about flexibility now. (<a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47610346.\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Gift link</em></a>.)</li></ul><p>💡 <strong>But boutiques still have an edge </strong>and plenty of dedicated consumers booking classes. Specialized studios can offer the expert instructors, identity and community that made the model successful in the first place.</p><ul><li><strong>\"Boutique is not going anywhere,\"</strong> LaBazzo added, because a gym offering many things can't be as singularly focused on one experience.</li></ul><p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> Fitness may not be fully rebundled yet. But gyms increasingly want to give you fewer reasons to leave.</p></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47610346.373567/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtM2VhNjk4MGUtMTcwMy00YWI2LTk2ZTAtYTAyYmNmOGQxMWNmLmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkxJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZm